{
  "version": 1,
  "description": "Backup file (JSON export/import). Three kinds of case; full rules in README.md. kind 'serialize': input { kind, exportedAtUtc: date-time in UTC, bills: [Bill], payments: [Payment], snapshots: [Snapshot], settings: { key: string or null } } -> expected { document }, the backup file parsed as JSON and compared structurally (key order and whitespace do not matter). The document has formatVersion 1, exportedAt as the UTC time with 7 fractional digits and a Z, then bills, payments, snapshots, settings; keys are camelCase; a property whose value is null is left out, but a null settings value is written as null. kind 'parse': input { kind, document: object } or { kind, text: string } (text is used where the input is not a plain JSON object, such as invalid JSON or duplicate keys). expected { backup } or { rejected: true }. backup is normalised: every field of every record is present, missing fields take their defaults (Bill defaults in README.md; Payment: id 0, billId '', periodKey '', amountPaid 0; Snapshot: id 0, snapshotDate '', totalRemaining 0; optional strings null), missing bills/payments/snapshots/settings are empty. Keys are case-sensitive camelCase; unknown keys are ignored. Legacy bills: keys 'cost' and 'owed' inside a bill are read as 'payment' and 'remaining'. Rejected: not valid JSON, a root that is not an object, formatVersion missing or < 1 or > 1, or a value of the wrong JSON type. kind 'roundTrip': input as for 'serialize' -> expected { backup }, the normalised result of parsing what serialize wrote.",
